\section{Interpreting the spread}{

\code{\link{sim_mod}} redraws the observations only, and warns about each row
it leaves alone: a predator with empirical rather than estimated suitability
has no predicted diet to draw from, and a covariance (MVN/MVNORM) survey
fleet has no covariance outside its fitted years. Those data are identical in
every replicate, so recovery of whatever they inform is optimistic.

By default recruitment is not redrawn, so under \code{random_rec = TRUE}
every replicate shares the operating model's single realization -- its shrunk
empirical-Bayes modes, not a draw from N(0, sigmaR). The spread across
replicates is then observation error alone and a lower bound on estimation
uncertainty in SSB and recruitment, so do not read it against the model's own
uncertainty bands, which include process error; and sigmaR is re-estimated
from deviations shrunk toward zero the same way every time, a downward bias
averaging does not remove. \code{process = "recruitment"} removes both, at
the cost of asking a different question. See
\code{vignette("model-diagnostics")}.
}

